{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Behold, we were binding sheaves, etc.</b> They perceived by their wisdom that the interpretation of the dream of the sheaves — that they were bowing to his sheaf — signified the ascendancy of kingship, and this is what is meant by ״וגם נצבה״ (and it remained standing), which is of the language of kingship, in the manner of what is written (I Kings 22:48) ״נצב מלך״. And because they knew this through the wisdom of dream interpretation, they said to him as if mocking: ״המלוך תמלוך עלינו אם משול תמשול בנו״ — 'Will you indeed reign over us? Or will you indeed rule over us?' But it was hidden from them that through grain (תבואה) the kingship was destined to come to him. Now it is well known that since they hated him it would have been fitting for them to interpret it for the worse, but it was all from the Holy One, blessed be He, for He arranged the interpretation of kingship in their mouths. And behold, this is like the matter spoken of concerning Gideon (Judges 7:14): ״אין זאת בלתי אם חרב גדעון בן יואש איש ישראל״ — 'This is none other than the sword of Gideon son of Joash, a man of Israel.' For the Holy One, blessed be He, had said: ״רד אל מחנה מדין״ — 'Go down to the camp of Midian' — ״ואם ירא אתה לרדת רד אתה ופורה נערך אל המחנה״ — 'and if you are afraid to go down, go down with Purah your lad to the camp.' And when he went down to the camp of Midian, he heard one man of the camp of Midian recounting a dream to his fellow, and he said to him: ״חלום חלמתי והנה צליל לחם שעורים מתהפך במחנה מדין״ — 'I had a dream, and behold, a cake of barley bread was tumbling into the camp of Midian' — and his fellow answered him: ״אין זאת בלתי אם חרב גדעון בן יואש איש ישראל״. The Holy One, blessed be He, arranged the interpretation in the mouth of his fellow so that Gideon would hear the dream and its interpretation and be strengthened by it — and so he did. So too here, in the matter of Joseph, the Holy One, blessed be He, arranged the interpretation in the mouths of his brothers to bring him to kingship, and that is what they said: ״המלוך תמלוך עלינו״." "You will find in the verse three occurrences of <b>והנה</b> (behold), corresponding to the three times they needed to go down to Egypt to bow down to my sheaf. And they had humbling before him: the first time when there was famine in the land of Canaan; the second time when Jacob said to them (Genesis 43:2) ״שובו שברו לנו מעט אוכל״ — 'Go back, buy us a little food' — at which point Joseph was revealed to them; and the third time when they returned to him with Jacob their father, and then all the dreams were fulfilled." "<b>And bow down to my sheaf.</b> Also the sun and the moon and the eleven stars bowed to him, and they are Jacob and Bilhah and his eleven brothers, in the manner of what is written concerning his brothers: ״וישתחוו לו אפים ארצה״, and it is written concerning Jacob (Genesis 47:31): ״וישתחו ישראל על ראש המטה״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {0 "The citation מלכים א כ״ב:מ״ח likely refers to I Kings 22:48 in the Hebrew verse numbering, which corresponds to I Kings 22:47 in English numbering; the verse contains the phrase נצב מלך."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:18:18.514331791Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}